          deck: 'Short deck: 24 cards (2 players) through 36 cards (5 players), made by removing low cards from a standard 52-card pack.',
          equipment: 'Cards and chips.',
          dealing: 'First dealer chosen at random; deal rotates left. Before each deal all players ante an equal amount. Cards are shuffled, cut, and dealt clockwise one at a time until each player has five cards.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Win the pot by having the highest-ranked five-card hand at showdown or by being the last player left after all others fold.',
          gameplay: '1. Ante: All players post an equal ante. 2. Deal: Five cards each, one at a time, clockwise. 3. First betting round: Minimum to open is a pair of Kings; players with less must pass; opener may pass. Once someone opens, others may call, raise, or fold. 4. Draw: Starting left of dealer, each player may exchange 0–4 cards (discard face down, receive replacements from stock). 5. Second betting round: Opener (or next active player) may check or bet; others fold, call, or raise. 6. Showdown: Remaining players reveal hands; highest hand wins. If nobody opens, hands are thrown in, pot stays, players ante again and the next dealer deals.',
          keyRules: [
            'Opening requirement: pair of Kings (oyunu açmak); less must pass (pas).',
            'Draw: 0–4 cards exchanged; if requesting four, only three given initially then the fourth after all others served; discards shuffled and reused if stock runs out.',
            'Hand order differs from international: flush beats full house; three of a kind beats straight; Ace can be low in minor straight; suits break ties (Hearts > Spades > Diamonds > Clubs).',
            'Opener who folds or wins without showdown may be required to show cards to prove legal open (pair of Kings or better).',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Only open with a pair of Kings or better; fold weak hands before the draw; pay attention to how many cards opponents draw.',
          intermediate: 'Use position: opening from late position allows you to see more passes; drawing one card can represent a strong hand (straight or flush draw) and support bluffing.',
          advanced: 'Track suit ranking for tiebreaks; in marginal spots consider whether your hand benefits from the Turkish order (e.g. flush over full house); defend openers selectively when you suspect a bluff.',
          tips: [
            'If you request four cards, you receive three first and the fourth after everyone else is served.',
            'Suit order for ties: Hearts (kupa) > Spades (maça) > Diamonds (karo) > Clubs (sinek).',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
